# Lab 02: Windows Failed Logon Analysis with Splunk

## Objective
To collect Windows Security event logs in Splunk and identify failed logon attempts (Event ID 4625) that I generated by deliberately entering a wrong password on my own machine.

## Lab Environment
- Host: Windows laptop (8 GB RAM, HDD)
- SIEM: Splunk Enterprise 10.6.0.5 (60-day trial licence), installed locally
- Data source: local Windows Event Logs (Application, Security, System)
- Destination index: main
- Test date: 15 October 2026

## Methodology
1. Installed Splunk Enterprise on the Windows host and logged in at localhost:8000.
2. Added the local Windows event logs under Settings > Data inputs > Local event log collection.
3. Confirmed collection with this search:
```
   index=main source="WinEventLog:Security"
```
4. Locked the screen and entered an incorrect password three times, then signed in normally.
5. Searched for failed logons:
```
   index=main source="WinEventLog:Security" EventCode=4625
```
6. Built a table of the results:
```
   index=main source="WinEventLog:Security" EventCode=4625
   | table _time, Account_Name, Logon_Type, Failure_Reason, Source_Network_Address
```

## Findings
- Splunk collected 236 Security events in a 60-minute window.
- The log included Event ID 4624 (successful logon) and 4672 (special privileges assigned to a new logon).
- Three Event ID 4625 (failed logon) events were recorded at 15:47:37, 15:47:41 and 15:47:45, about four seconds apart. This matches the three wrong-password attempts I made.
- Details from an expanded 4625 event: Account Name: [FILL IN]. Logon Type: [FILL IN]. Failure Reason: [FILL IN].

## Analysis
Event ID 4625 records one failed logon, and 4624 records a successful one. Here, a few failures a few seconds apart on my own machine, followed by a successful logon, is consistent with a user mistyping a password.

A brute-force attempt would look different: many 4625 events in a short window, often from one source address or aimed at several account names. A basic detection rule would count failures per account or source over a time window and flag a count above a threshold, for example more than five in five minutes. I did not build or test an alert in this lab.

## Limitations
- This was a controlled test on my own machine with one user and no real attacker.
- Only three failed logons were generated, so there is no baseline over time.
- Alerting was not configured or tested.
- The results show what Splunk recorded in this test. They do not show how the same rule would perform on a real network.

## Lessons Learned
- How to install Splunk and add Windows event logs as a data source.
- How to search by index, source and event code.
- What Event IDs 4624, 4625 and 4672 mean.
- How to tell a mistyped password from a brute-force pattern.
- Why detection depends on volume, timing and source, not on single events.

